## Context

Child of 0700, and **the part that distinguishes this proposal from an ordinary
bibliometric review**. It was promised to the editor by name, so it does not
shrink to a paragraph if the schedule gets tight.

Question: to what extent does the research literature take up the accounting
controversies that dominate the public-policy debate, and where does it develop
its own questions instead of following the policy agenda.

The four, as announced:

1. **Loan valuation** — at what value a concessional loan counts.
2. **Credibility of markers** — what the Rio markers measure.
3. **Attribution of private finance** — which private finance is mobilised by which public finance.
4. **Additionality** — what is added to existing aid.

## Upstream material already gathered (outside the repo)

Gathered since 2026-09-11 during the Gavard & Schoch audit, and directly useful
to controversy 2 (credibility of markers) as well as to the underlying
effectiveness question. EDM discipline: PDFs stay in staging, Zotero is the
system of record. The relevant records may enter the v3 bibliometric corpus
through its pipeline; the PDFs are not copied into this repo.

**Source note.** `~/CNRS/papiers/actif/Gavard Scotch 2026 Climate finance
counterproductive/audit/research-note-gavard-schoch-2026.md` — a standalone
audit note on Gavard & Schoch (2026). It distinguishes Rio mitigation, Rio
adaptation and CDM transfers; reconstructs the staggered-lag GEE design from the
published Stata code; flags a coefficient-to-percentage conversion error (1.76%
rather than 4.7% for adaptation; 1.11% rather than 1.86% for mitigation) — **to
be checked against the published PDF before any public allegation**, since the
Zenodo archive dates from 2022. It also records the replication caveats: 164/168
country gap, manual Excel aggregation, mislabelled `perGDP` variables,
AR(4)/AR(7) inconsistency.

The subject has become a full paper project in its own right (Comment for
*EJW*, `manuscript/` and `reproduction/` in the same folder). The note was first
proposed to the repo as PR #1323, closed on 2026-09-11: it is not source-note
material for this repo, only a reference for it.

**Article batch.** Same folder, `docs/` — fourteen articles plus the Li–Rishi–Bae
supplement, on the effect of climate finance and green aid on emissions:
Apergis–Pinar–Ünlü 2024, Aquilas–Atemnkeng 2022, Bai et al. 2022,
Gavard–Schoch 2021 (ZEW DP 21-014), Han–Jun 2023, Lee et al. 2022,
Li–Rishi–Bae 2021 (+ supplement), Li et al. 2022, Lin et al. 2023, Mahalik
et al. 2021, Ming et al. 2025, Nghiem et al. 2026, Pinar 2023, Wu–Pan–She 2021.

The list above describes the initial batch. As of 2026-09-25, `docs/` contains
28 PDFs, supplements included, and the local Zotero library has 28
bibliographic records across the audit and comment collections. Fourteen DOIs
are found in `refined_works.csv`, fourteen are not; among the 24 records dated
2024 or earlier, twelve do not match by DOI. Matching by title and the scope
decision remain to be done: these twelve missing DOIs do not prove twelve
missing works. The published version of Gavard & Schoch and Zeng et al. are now
in `docs/`; Wang & Tang remains flagged in `docs/missing-papers.html` with no
local PDF.

**Status of the batch.** It results from a targeted search: its content alone
does not measure the presence or absence of a theme in the literature. It serves
to detect and investigate coverage gaps in the v3 corpus before measuring on a
documented REL revision (0700/0702).

## Upstream material (continued, 2026-09-23)

The Gavard & Schoch audit went down to the project lines of the authors' OECD
extraction (Zenodo 7149667). All of it is outside the repo, in
`~/CNRS/papiers/actif/Gavard Scotch 2026 Climate finance counterproductive/audit/`:

- `provider-check-2026-09-23/`: coverage by provider and by year. The MDBs
  (including IBRD, IDA, IFC) and the GEF report to the DAC only from the 2013
  flows. The multilateral share of mitigation goes from 0% (until 2008) to
  40–54% (2013–2017). Two counting rules coexist: Rio markers at face value,
  MDB climate components (median 5% of the loan).
- `provider-sensitivity-2026-09-23/`: re-estimations on consistent provider
  sets, with an adversarial review (`REVIEW.md`).
- `climate-finance-composition-2026-09-23/REPORT.md`: sectoral composition and
  verified bibliography of the markers critique, directly useful to
  controversies 1 (loan valuation) and 2 (credibility of markers).
- `ReproductibilityNotes.md`, RN-011: 40% weighting announced but not applied
  (the paper believes the OECD base is already weighted), swap of the two
  Congos, mislabelled table A2.

For controversy 2, the case illustrates a pattern to establish across the whole
batch (0863): effectiveness studies take over measurement conventions without
checking them. Gavard & Schoch's own errors go through their corrigendum, a
letter sent on 2026-09-23. The review cites the pattern, not the errors.

## Actions

1. Have 0700/0702 handle the concordance of the gathered references and the
   relevant gaps in the v3 corpus; distinguish context or method texts out of
   scope from texts later than the REL window.
2. For each controversy, measure presence on the REL revision of the corpus:
   volume, period, currents carrying it, venues.
3. Read what research says about it, and distinguish it from what the policy
   debate says.
4. Write the gap finding — even if it is weak: a field that follows the policy
   agenda is a result, not a failure of the measurement.
5. Name the questions of its own that the literature develops outside this agenda.

## Invariants

- The data `data/book/riomarkers/` belongs to the book chapter, **not** to this
  manuscript — do not import it here without arbitration.
- A negative finding ("the literature does not take it up") is the most fragile
  claim to write: support it with a measurement on the corpus, not with a
  reading impression.

## Exit criteria

- [ ] Presence of the four controversies measured on the corpus
- [ ] Section written, research/policy-debate gap established or refuted
- [ ] The literature's own questions named
